 respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167131 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d42bef6-ce15-55df-885d-72410b164f1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167782 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167782 #edb/ref 1061 657078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167782 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88355efb-c547-50b1-b76e-a290fd494387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167782 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167783 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167783 #edb/ref 1061 657079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167783 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c8b0ad8-3378-5dfc-8d4a-545a7208b294" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167783 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167784 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186362446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167784 #edb/ref 1061 657083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167784 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "53d98073-01e7-5129-ad66-75d01c7d3e9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167784 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167785 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167785 #edb/ref 1061 657075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167785 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "507e9c0b-b2be-5bf9-a53c-e763d7e4315b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167785 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167786 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167786 #edb/ref 1061 657077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167786 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b9873f3-a548-5ea9-a86b-7682d0efa06b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167786 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167787 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167787 #edb/ref 1061 657076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167787 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15c5dca7-7c90-57e5-be91-60bf5e44e76b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167787 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167788 #edb/ref 1061 657087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167788 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99f84857-19d8-532f-b1f8-974d1ed142df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167788 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167789 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167789 #edb/ref 1061 657082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167789 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e8905e0e-c7f6-5708-9e74-d2d69c659271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167789 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168144 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle xz + y,\\: x^2,\\: 2y-z^2 \\right\\rangle\\!, \n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{n}(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle 2,\\: 0,\\: -1 \\right\\rangle\\!.\n$$\n\nLet's construct a surface integral representing the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}.$\n\nFirst, we recall that if $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨xz+y,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨xz+y,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}((xz+y)\\cdot 2+{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+(2y-{z}^{2})\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(2xz+2y-2y+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2xz+{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral representing the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf n$ can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2xz+{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{∬}_{S}2xz+{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can then evaluate this integral using the usual methods." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168144 #edb/ref 1065 7632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168144 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c936101d-fbb9-594d-888c-2327d0908e71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168144 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168145 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the flux of a two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ across a curve $C$ with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n(x,y)$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nThe flux measures the total flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/23/2392d6c27fc6767c78172936d6df37b3dfec8b2198abbdf9e22b87d4337ed9da.png)\n\nFlux is always measured with respect to a preselected unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and we have two choices of orientation for this vector. If we measure the flux relative to the unit normal pointing in the *opposite* direction, our flux will have the *same* magnitude but *opposite* sign.\n\n![](images/e4/e451548a5d9874b9eff9d61c2d423696dc76a3f560e2936eb7bdb3a9cbfbed49.png)\n\nThis lesson aims to extend the concept of flux to three-dimensional vector fields. But first, we need a brief discussion on surface orientations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168145 #edb/ref 1065 7725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168145 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8a17f47e-f50d-578f-a41c-9e537ded3620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168145 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168146 #edb/ref 1064 "In three dimensions, flux measures the total flow of a vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ *through an orientable surface.*\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is a three-dimensional vector field, and $S$ is an orientable surface with the choice of orientation given by the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9976f35dcf348da89c15a40aad43ab5a1d3edea75558ef5539f9421ed9b75f4e.png)\n\nThe **flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $\\boldsymbol{S}$** with respect to $\\mathbf n,$ which we write as \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf S\n$$\n\n is given by the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n \\,\\textrm d S.\n$$\n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare this to the flux formula for two-dimensional vector fields: \n\n- The quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ evaluated at a point $P$ on $S$ measures the projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf n$ at $P.$\n\n- By summing (integrating) the quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ over the entire surface, we get the total flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nOur flux depends on our choice of orientation. If we were to select the *opposite* orientation, we'd get a result with the same magnitude but opposite sign.\n\nAs in the case of two-dimensional vector fields, we have a choice between two possible normal vectors at each point. For instance, we could have picked the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction, as shown below.\n\n<div id=\"graphic-b46ee2d2f4637027c6704e244e0b2f96\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/b46ee2d2f4637027c6704e244e0b2f96\" /></div> \n\nThe key takeaway is that when calculating the flux of a vector field across a surface, we must be clear about which orientation we're measuring our flux relative to." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168146 #edb/ref 1065 7727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168146 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5a72f9c0-0bf4-59dd-be49-827f9e278fa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168146 #edb/ref 1067 "Flux in Three-Dimensional Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168147 #edb/ref 1064 "A surface is **orientable** if it has two distinct sides. So, for example, the surface $S$ shown below is orientable.\n\n![](images/29/29be1e415eaf0072aea7f4093e71665f5996b29804702a37a4d4493e0b0002d4.png)\n\nIf a unit vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z)$ to our orientable surface varies continuously over $S,$ we say that $S$ is **oriented** and the choice of $\\mathbf n$ gives an **orientation** of $S.$\n\nWe can choose the opposite orientation for our surface by selecting a vector that points in the opposite direction, as shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40268c448b7c4ac99acf356e9f1e2702112a0d46dc1b6e6afd98d5c95e5ea317.png)\n\nWhen dealing with two-sided (i.e., orientable) surfaces, we must always be clear about our chosen orientation. \n\nNote that some surfaces are *not* orientable. This means they do not have two distinct sides. An example of a non-orientable surface is a Möbius strip, shown below.\n\n![](images/27/277ffb9e1594e64f3411b0c5063841616aaa433ccc4915bc96e2c9ab865ece95.png)\n\nA Möbius strip can be made by taking a long rectangular piece of paper, twisting one of its ends by $180^\\circ$ and gluing the two ends together. \n\nA Möbius strip does not have two distinct sides because if you were to start at a point $P$ on the strip and move along it (following the dark blue curve shown), you'd eventually end up at the same point but on the opposite side! Thus, a Möbius strip only has one distinct side. \n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider orientable surfaces." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168147 #edb/ref 1065 7728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168147 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e93caa6c-3516-5696-8b97-769b20ec4ac0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168147 #edb/ref 1067 "Orientable vs. Non-Orientable Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168148 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have the horizontal plane $z=k,$ where $k$ is a fixed parameter. \n\nThe magnitude of the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}|| & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we have that\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf r_x =  \\langle 1, 0, 0\\rangle = \\mathbf i, \\qquad \\mathbf r_y =  \\langle 0, 1, 0\\rangle = \\mathbf j.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf r_x \\times\\mathbf r_y = \\mathbf i\\times\\mathbf j = \\mathbf k\n\\]\n\nand therefore\n\n\\[\n|| \\mathbf r_x \\times\\mathbf r_y|| = ||\\mathbf k|| =1. \n\\]\n\nTherefore, in such cases, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,f(x,y,z)\\text{d}S\\, & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRestating, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z)\\, \\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nWe arrive at a similar result when considering vertical planes parallel to the $xz$- or $yz$-axes.\n\nThis observation can help provide a shortcut when calculating the flux of a vector field through a horizontal plane. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168148 #edb/ref 1065 7733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168148 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3a45ce20-02a0-56e8-9b38-add2ba9480d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168148 #edb/ref 1067 "The Fundamental Vector Product for a Horizontal Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321447 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321447 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321447 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2675eb0f-c25d-53b2-81e3-8fb563f36009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322095 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322095 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322095 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "78932147-1c07-5243-a33b-54ccc3b3d246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322678 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322678 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322678 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c5320cee-9b68-5526-9f7b-e4e81b3395ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322820 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322820 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322820 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d670b8fa-5cf4-557e-9b86-650409c1e001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322865 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322865 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322865 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db414795-e6be-52ec-a2e8-11da08ea2311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323358 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323358 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323358 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323358 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323358 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323358 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323358 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323358 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186322865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323358 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5f36e3b8-2489-5327-a723-3c371eeae8a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-365879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323829 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323829 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,4x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7134c822-9a7e-5b0a-ae0e-9d8056e17711" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323829 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,4x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,y,\\,4x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}4x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {[2{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362413 #edb/ref 1064 "If we wish to find the flux through a tilted plane, we should first calculate the unit normal vector to this plane.\n\nFor example, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle x,\\: 1-x+y,\\: 2y+8\\rangle$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=2x+2y-1$ such that $\\textrm{Area}(S) = 2.$ \nAssume that we need to find the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$\n\nIf $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z = 2x+2y-1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = -1, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,1-x+y,\\,2y+8⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,1-x+y,\\,2y+8⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2x-2(1-x+y)+2y+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2x-2+2x-2y+2y+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362413 #edb/ref 1065 15974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362413 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "48c45790-f4c7-562f-9a3f-46bf6009dd4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362413 #edb/ref 1067 "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362446 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362446 #edb/ref 1061 657086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362446 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "81d0dc6b-4e76-5f80-934b-e300c50b0201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362446 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-365884" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370853 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c5b1199-265f-51a1-b6b8-07c045672fdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370853 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 3,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370854 #edb/ref 1075 #ed